## Self-Heal: Failure Analyst Summary
Root cause: Tests expect import('./cli') to reject, but async main() auto-invoked at module bottom causes unhandled rejection, not import rejection.
Fix: Do NOT use `await expect(import('./cli')).rejects.toThrow()` for async CLI entry points — in CommonJS the import resolves before the async rejection propagates. Instead: (1) guard auto-invocation in cli.ts with `if (require.main === module) { main().catch(console.error); }` so importing the module does NOT trigger main(); (2) import { main } from './cli' and test it directly with `await expect(main()).rejects.toThrow('process.exit called with code: 2')`. Set process.argv before each call.
## Self-Heal: Failure Analyst Summary
Root cause: Tests use import('./cli').rejects pattern; CommonJS import resolves before async process.exit throws, causing unhandled rejection.
Fix: Do NOT use `await expect(import('./cli')).rejects.toThrow()`. Instead: (1) guard auto-invocation in cli.ts with `if (require.main === module) main()`; (2) import { main } from './cli'; (3) call `await main()` directly with spied process.argv/process.exit/console.error; (4) assert exitSpy was called with expected code and stderr contains expected message.
